# The oracle image, pinned.
#
# One line per reference, tab-separated: <name> <image-ref> <version> <what it
# answers>. Read by tools/oracle/oracle_env.py, which splits on tabs and takes
# three fields. It is machine-read, not documentation.
#
# All five names share one image, which is the shape this library needs and not
# the shape `unicode` and `font` have: a single `make test` reaches for several
# of these references, sometimes in one test binary, so five images would be
# five things to keep in step for no gain. notes/suite/CONTAINERS.md section 4a
# is the argument for having an image here at all - the problem it solves is
# *absence*, not drift. Every reference below is present on the machine this
# was written on, which is why twenty-one tests that reach for one have never
# yet failed to find it, and why the skip they would print reads as success.
#
# Two kinds of pin and they are not the same guarantee (CONTAINERS.md section
# 2.6). This image is **built here**, so there is no digest to trust: what is
# pinned is everything its Containerfile reads, and the run-time version check
# is the real guarantee. `oracle_env.check_pin()` therefore runs in **both**
# modes, as in `font` and unlike `unicode`, because no line below names a stock
# image whose digest could stand in for the check.
#
# Raising a pin is deliberate. A newer Pillow is newer answers, and a new
# answer that disagrees is a finding to triage rather than a regression to fix.
#
# Building it:
#   podman build -t ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-3 \
#       -f tools/oracle/containers/image-refs/Containerfile tools/oracle
# `make oracle-build` does exactly that. The tag says deb13 because the base is
# what decides four of the five versions, and -1 is a revision that moves when
# the contents do; `:latest` is never a tag here.

# The three decoders that come from Debian at a pinned version.
#
# libjpeg-turbo is reached two ways - through Pillow and through
# tests/tools/jpeg-oracle - and inside this image both spellings are the same
# 2.1.5, which is a change from the host where Pillow's pip wheel bundles its
# own. jpeg_test_utils.cpp tries Pillow first and the dumper otherwise; that
# fallback can no longer change the answer.
pillow	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-3	Pillow 11.1.0	Decoded pixels for JPEG, and the reader every verify_*_output.py checks our output with
libjpeg	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-3	libjpeg-turbo 2.1.5	Decoded pixels and DCT coefficients, through tests/tools/jpeg-oracle
giflib	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-3	giflib 5.2.2	LZW expansion, interlace order, colour tables and geometry - not compositing

# The other two BMP decoders tests/data/bmp/bmpsuite_sweep.py corroborates
# with. They arrived a tag after the rest, because `make bmpsuite` is not part
# of `make test` and so nothing in a routine build had ever asked them
# anything - which is the same blindness in a different place: the survey that
# found twenty-one skipped comparisons was reading the test binaries, and this
# target is not one.
pixbuf	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-3	GdkPixbuf 2.42.12	A third reading of every BMP in the suite
netpbm	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-3	netpbm 11.10.2	A fourth, through bmptopnm

# The two that are a git checkout rather than a release. Their version field is
# `@VERSIONS`, which means the pin lives in tools/oracle/VERSIONS and is read
# from there.
#
# Deliberately an indirection rather than a copy. Those two commits were pinned
# long before this image existed, VERSIONS says why in its own words, and
# fetch.sh reads it with `awk '$1 == name { print $2; exit }'` - first match
# wins. CONTAINERS.md section 2.2: a pin written in two files is a pin that can
# disagree with itself, and the disagreement is silent.
#
# What the image adds to them is the other half of the pin. A commit says which
# *source*; it does not say which compiler built it. "bmplib agrees on 79 of 91
# files" named a decoder built by whatever the host had until now.
bmplib	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-3	@VERSIONS	OS/2 Huffman 1D, bitmap arrays and 64-bit BMPs - the formats no installed decoder reads
bmpsuite	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-3	@VERSIONS	The 91-file conformance corpus tests/data/bmp/bmpsuite_sweep.py runs

# A GIF reader that is neither giflib nor this library, and the only one here
# that composites a whole animation rather than handing back stored frames.
#
# It arrived a tag late and the reason is worth keeping: verify_gif_output.py
# asks four decoders and drops any it cannot import, and its summary line names
# a frame count and not its decoders - so when that script moved into this
# image without ImageMagick in it, three read exactly like four and the run
# stayed green. The script now names what answered.
imagemagick	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-3	ImageMagick 7.1.1-43	A whole GIF animation, composited, by a reader independent of giflib

# Not a comparison oracle. tests/data/jpeg/generate.py writes an EXIF
# orientation with it, so a fixture's bytes depend on it - pinned for the
# reason `font` pins its fontTools (CONTAINERS.md section 7), which is that a
# generator's version is part of what its output means.
piexif	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-3	piexif 1.1.3	The EXIF writer tests/data/jpeg/generate.py builds its orientation fixture with

# The two built from source in the image, pinned in tools/oracle/VERSIONS
# under the upstream project's own name rather than the name used here.
#
# libjpeg-turbo 3.0.4 is a *second* libjpeg, beside Debian's 2.1.5 and not
# instead of it: 2.1.5 is what Pillow links and what every eight-bit
# comparison is measured against, and 3.x is the one carrying the 8-, 12- and
# 16-bit codecs in one library. It is the only reference this library has ever
# had for its twelve-bit path, and until now it existed only as a paragraph of
# build instructions - so the "352 of 352 byte-exact" figure in
# tests/data/jpeg/README.md named a binary that had to be rebuilt by hand.
#
# IJG v10's pin is a SHA-256 of the tarball rather than a commit, because
# there is no repository, and VERSIONS says plainly that the digest is ours
# and not one IJG publishes.
#
# **It answers nothing yet, and the reason it was wanted was wrong.** This
# file said, and the Makefile said before it, that IJG v10 is "the only
# reference here for SOF3 lossless and for arithmetic coding". Measured over
# all 151 JPEG fixtures on 2026-09-25, both halves are false:
#
#   arithmetic  libjpeg-turbo 2.1.5 reads all six arithmetic fixtures. So
#               does IJG. It is not unique.
#   lossless    neither reads one. IJG v10 answers `Unsupported JPEG process:
#               SOF type 0xc3` to every SOF3 file here.
#
#   reads       IJG 73 of 151, libjpeg-turbo 2.1.5 88 of 151, and the set IJG
#               reads is a strict *subset* - it adds no file at all.
#
# And on the 73 they share it is not a second opinion either: IJG v10 and
# libjpeg-turbo 3.0.4 disagree on 37 of them. 25 of those are the chroma
# upsampling filter, which IJG changed after 6b (`-nosmooth` on both sides
# takes the disagreement to 12); the remaining 12 are not yet explained.
#
# So it is pinned, reachable and reproducible - which is what was asked for -
# and it is *not* wired to any comparison, because a reference that disagrees
# with the one already here on half of what it reads is a piece of work to
# characterise, not an oracle to switch on. The 12 unexplained files are where
# that work starts.
libjpeg12	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-3	@VERSIONS:libjpeg-turbo	The 12- and 16-bit JPEG codecs, through cjpeg-static and djpeg-static
ijg10	localhost/ghoti-image-oracle-refs:deb13-3	@VERSIONS:jpeg-10	Pinned and reachable; answers nothing yet - see the note above for what it does and does not read

# Not here, and each is its own piece of work:
#   libpng      a second reading of every PNG this library writes; Pillow is
#               the reader today and it cannot see bKGD at all, so the gate it
#               anchors is narrower than it looks (memory: png-bkgd-oracles)
